deep.reconstruction:
This folio is a printed page (p. 6) of an elementary German arithmetic textbook in Fraktur, bound into Gauss's papers. The only handwriting on the page itself is a small interlinear insertion above 'die eine', apparently 'gleich', which turns the phrase into 'ob die eine gleich so groß, größer oder kleiner sey'. The handwritten formulas at the right edge are not on this leaf. They are the edge of the facing manuscript page (the 'NEXT PAGE'), which holds Gauss's Latin notes on Newton's identities. So there is no Gauss derivation on this page.

Printed content, restated:

(i) End of §4 (numeration). The grouped numeral 3|467''|891|076'|384|602 is read as 'three thousand four hundred sixty-seven billions, eight hundred ninety-one thousand and seventy-six millions, three hundred eighty-four thousand six hundred and two'. This uses the long scale, where Billion = 10^12. The value is 3467*10^12 + 891076*10^6 + 384602 = 3,467,891,076,384,602. Point 5) states that a unit of a lower decimal class is a (decimal) fraction of a unit of the next higher class.

(ii) §5 (ratios). A comparison of two homogeneous magnitudes is a 'Verhältnis'. It is arithmetic if it concerns only the difference, a-b, and geometric if it concerns how many times one contains the other, a:b. The signs introduced are:
- '=' for equality;
- '>' for greater/less, with the opening toward the larger;
- '-' for difference or subtraction;
- '+' for addition;
- ':' for division in a geometric ratio.

Examples given:
- A = B, C > A, C - A = D, illustrated by line segments C (a scale with unit ticks), A, B, D, E;
- 15 - 6 = 9 as an arithmetic ratio;
- AB compared with the unit F, written 14:1, as a geometric ratio. Every number gives such a ratio to unity.

(iii) §6. Large and small are only relative: a fly is large compared with a cheese mite and small compared with an elephant.

All of this is routine 18th-century school arithmetic.
